  "caveat": "Replay of DFS Upwards/System requests from 7 Oct 2026, 13 scored days. Gross settlement is what NESO would pay at the bid price if the bid were accepted and delivered (pay-as-bid assumed; a bid is assumed accepted whenever it is at or below a price NESO actually accepted that half-hour). Electricity cost uses a market index price, not any site's tariff, and excludes levies. At the assumed site value the settlement (GBP 10,500) does not cover the electricity (GBP 16,091) on its own; the site's value for the energy makes up the difference. See `sensitivity` for other values. Full delivery is assumed, which is optimistic: NESO's published settled cost is only 39% of accepted cost across DFS so far. Only 28 half-hours were tradable for zone Z1 across these days (a bid had actually been accepted there), so percentages of the oracle rest on a handful of events and 'ours matches the oracle' at low site values is a small-sample effect. Not a revenue claim. DFS zone IDs are not confirmed as Scotland."
 },
 "assumptions": {
  "settlement": "pay-as-bid (arithmetic checked against NESO accepted cost), perfect delivery assumed",
  "acceptance": "bid price at or below the highest price NESO accepted in the load's own zone in that half-hour; none accepted in that zone = not tradable",
  "energy_price": "volume-weighted market index price per half-hour",
  "naive_rule": "bid a fixed price in the cheapest-electricity half-hours; the price is the median accepted-price ceiling in the load's zone on earlier days only (GBP 60 with no history)",
  "ours_rule": "rank half-hours by expected margin = P(accepted at price) x (price + value - electricity price) x MWh; P(accepted) is learned from earlier days by electricity-price and boundary-utilisation tercile",
  "oracle_rule": "hindsight best tradable half-hours, each bid at its accepted price ceiling",
  "window_local": "11:00 to 23:00"
 },
